<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  096 review 11A — the App Group, for a TEST RUNNER rather than for a shipping
	  process, and the reason is worth stating because a test target holding an
	  entitlement looks like a mistake at a glance.

	  `Tier2ShareUITests` asserts that a share from Safari reached the inbox. Until now
	  it asserted that by reading a COUNT off the app's own export control — which is
	  true whenever a capture landed, and stays true when tier 2 has silently degraded
	  to tier 1: a preprocessing script that threw, a plist boundary that broke the way
	  422 describes, or a media fetch that failed all produce a capture, all increment
	  the count, and all mean the feature under test did not work.

	  What separates those cases is the RECORD: its platform, its originalURL, whether
	  it has a payload. That record is a file in the App Group container, and a test
	  process without this entitlement cannot see the container at all —
	  `containerURL(forSecurityApplicationGroupIdentifier:)` simply returns nil.

	  The alternative was a debug-only provenance surface in the app, built for one
	  test and shipped forever in the binary. The test's own header rejected that, and
	  it was right to: an entitlement on a target that never ships is the smaller cost.

	  $(ATELIER_APP_GROUP) is the same per-configuration build setting the app and the
	  share extension use — Debug `group.sujenphea.AtelierRefs.dev`, Release
	  `group.sujenphea.AtelierRefs`. Fed from one place so the container this runner is
	  GRANTED and the one it ASKS for (via `AtelierAppGroupIdentifier` in this target's
	  Info.plist) cannot disagree.

	  READ ONLY, by discipline rather than by entitlement: the runner enumerates and
	  decodes records and never writes to the inbox. The writer is the share extension
	  and the drain is the Mac; a test that wrote here would be inventing a third
	  producer of a format two shipped binaries read.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>$(ATELIER_APP_GROUP)</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
